seg-7ce1d69e710e = Epochs the receiver itself says are not a satellite fix (GGA quality { "0" }, or { "6" } to { "8" }: estimated, manual, simulator; RMC status <c1/>, or mode <c2/>, <c3/>, <c4/>, <c5/>) are not used for the position checks and carry no evidence either way: the receiver has already said so.

# text
# c1: calibration_s
seg-a0ae80781c6e = The first <c1/> seconds form the baseline and are never scored. Every monitor looks only at the current and earlier epochs, so a decision at an epoch does not change when later data arrives, and a live stream gives the same score as the same text read as a file.

# text
seg-068593ad0a52 = What each check catches, and what it cannot

# text
seg-094b90f0c701 = Directions between fixes (the track bearing used by the turn-rate check, the course implied by two positions) are the direction of the chord between them, which is the geodesic's <b>mean azimuth</b> between the fixes, not the forward azimuth at the first fix; the two differ by about half the convergence of the meridians between the fixes ({ "0.001" } degrees for a { "260" } m baseline at { "59.5" } degrees latitude).

# text
seg-ed3cdfcfbff5 = A counterfeit position has to agree with a ship's physics and with the ship's other sensors. Each check compares the fix with one of them. A check is only as independent as its other source: a heading from a GNSS compass, or a "speed through the water" derived from GNSS, is not independent of the receiver under test and adds nothing.

# text
seg-9480aabf136e = Check

# text
seg-20e4f746c7e1 = Catches

# text
seg-e7b2ebe5eabc = Cannot

# text
seg-21ad9895f606 = a position step; a track whose movement the reported speed and course do not explain; an implied speed, acceleration or turn rate above the stated vessel limits

# text
seg-dcbc6d4cd17f = a drag whose reported speed and course follow the counterfeit track and stays inside the limits (the receiver derives them from its own solution, so they agree with each other); a speed limit stated too high

# text
seg-3542f83a7988 = a track that departs from where the ship points by more than the stated crab angle

# text
# c1: min_speed_kn
seg-75f4d659f716 = a drag along the course (the course does not change); a heading from the same receiver or a GNSS compass; a cross-set above the tolerance (false alarm); speeds below <c1/>

# text
seg-09dde82be447 = a drag that changes speed over ground against speed through the water

# text
seg-6ac740e5f51c = a log that is GNSS-derived; a current above the allowance (false alarm); a drag slower than the allowance

# text
seg-e85d5f0933b7 = an altitude that is not where the sea surface and the stated antenna height put it

# text
seg-3806e1d46332 = a spoofer that keeps the altitude plausible; the tolerance is wide because standalone vertical error is large; the geoid separation is recorded but not checked, because there is no geoid model in the engine

# text
seg-5b70bd80640e = the signature of one transmitter: C/N0 across the satellites collapsing together, or rising together, against the calibration baseline

# text
seg-e6874a8047b1 = a spoofer that shapes power per satellite; anything while the baseline itself is spoofed; a GSV cadence too slow to follow

# text
seg-371b17ce936a = the receiver's time stepping irregularly or running backwards; against this computer's clock, the receiver's time drifting from it (real-time streams only)

# text
seg-026479cb7966 = a counterfeit time that is consistent and steady; a clock that is wrong from the start

# text
seg-906ab911b73d = a reported OSNMA authentication failure

# text
seg-a04757b7ae57 = the status is read, not verified; a spoofer that suppresses the report is not seen

# text
seg-c36c24a337b8 = power denial and loss of satellites, as in the static case

# text
seg-940b39a3988a = they say the environment is hostile, not that the fix is wrong

