seg-39b3dfbd97cc = The <c1/> monitor takes per-satellite authentication status from an authentication source through <c2/> (a list of <c3/>, status <c4/>, <c5/> or <c6/>), next to the overall <c7/>. On the NMEA side the same is carried by <c8/>, for example <c9/>. Any failure, overall or on one satellite, alarms the monitor and costs its whole weight (it is given the ratio { "1.5" }); success is no evidence. Kshana verifies nothing here: the source that does is responsible for the status it reports.

# text
# c1: [live] idle_flush_s
# c2: calibration_s
# c3: time-consistency
# c4: calibration_s
seg-8ffc94901796 = An epoch is complete when the next timed sentence arrives; sentences of slower or unsynchronised instruments (a gyro a few tenths of a second behind the receiver) that arrive before then belong to it. If the stream stalls for <c1/> (default { "1.5" } s, set it above the longest gap between your receiver's timed sentences) the epoch is completed as it stands. The first <c2/> seconds of the stream form the baseline and are never scored, so <b>a stream that is already being spoofed when it is first connected has no clean baseline</b>: start the layer before the voyage, or while the fix is known to be good. The calibration window closes once, by arrival: an epoch whose time is earlier than a time already seen (a rewind, a replay of the stream's start) is never calibration whatever its time says; it is scored with a full <c3/> deduction, which is untrusted. The live engine scores each epoch over the calibration epochs and the last minute or so of the stream, so its cost per epoch grows with <c4/> times the epoch rate; keep the window short on a fast receiver. Its buffers are bounded (a flood of lines without a timed sentence is completed every { "5,000" } lines, calibration and history are capped at { "20,000" } epochs).

# text
# c1: --replay
# c2: --file
# c3: --follow
# c4: --file ... --follow
seg-2ef5aad7c750 = The receiver's time is also compared with this computer's monotonic clock, which only means something for a stream arriving in real time. A stored log fed in at full speed through stdin, TCP or UDP trips that check by construction: pass <c1/>. (<c2/> without <c3/> already implies it.) A <c4/> is read as a replay until it has been read to its end and the host clock is engaged from then on, so a backlog does not read as a clock fault; the check then needs the calibration window to still be open (otherwise it has no baseline and does not run).

# text
# c1: --udp <port>
# c2: --udp <addr>:<port>
seg-a06e58b2cfc8 = Stream ends and faults: stdin and a file read to its end finish normally (exit { "0" }). A TCP connection closed by the peer is not a normal end for a stream that should go on: the layer exits non-zero after scoring and writing everything it had received and does not reconnect, so run it under a supervisor that restarts it. UDP datagrams that split a sentence are joined; the readers of stdin, a file and TCP are backed by a bounded queue that pushes back on the sender (nothing is lost), while UDP, which cannot push back, drops datagrams the engine cannot take and says so on stderr. <c1/> binds the loopback address; give <c2/> to listen elsewhere (a warning is printed).

# text
# c1: $PKSHT
seg-0a0ed5e74ea4 = <c1/>: the proprietary sentence

# text
# c1: --pksht <path|->
seg-71ccf4a80c99 = Every epoch also produces one NMEA { "0183" } sentence, with a valid checksum, which <c1/> writes to a file or stdout and which the gate inserts into the forwarded stream:

# text
# c1: 1
seg-df9382f4b055 = <c1/>: the format version

# text
# c1: hhmmss.ss
seg-6986879f86fb = UTC time of the epoch, <c1/>; empty if the stream gave none

# text
seg-34418696f9d2 = trust score { "0" } to { "100" }, one decimal; empty while calibrating or when none could be produced

# text
# c1: C
# c2: N
# c3: D
# c4: U
seg-0b249ba73868 = band: <c1/> calibrating, <c2/> nominal, <c3/> degraded, <c4/> untrusted

# text
# c1: -
# c2: P
# c3: W
seg-281aba68405a = gate: <c1/> off, <c2/> passed, <c3/> withheld (the fix was marked invalid in the forwarded cycle)

# text
# c1: monitor:points
# c2: /
seg-f306c3f54a48 = up to two reasons, <c1/> joined by <c2/>; empty when nothing was deducted

# text
# c1: P
# c2: KSH
# c3: T
# c4: $PKSHT
seg-4bee29dfe578 = The address is <c1/> + the manufacturer mnemonic <c2/> + <c3/>. Equipment that does not know a proprietary sentence ignores it. Kshana ignores its own <c4/> on input.

# text
seg-d75c9da94f37 = Gate mode

# text
# c1: --gate
seg-30c1f4a5706c = <c1/> puts Kshana between the receiver and the equipment that uses its fix (a chart system, a track control system). The NMEA stream goes in, comes out on stdout, and is:

# text
# c1: $PKSHT
seg-aa0692146620 = <b>passed through unchanged</b> while the epoch is not untrusted (and during calibration), with a <c1/> after each cycle of sentences; and

# text
# c1: 0
# c2: V
# c3: N
# c4: V
# c5: N
# c6: V
# c7: N
# c8: N
seg-bbdd68edbb17 = <b>marked invalid</b> while it is untrusted: GGA quality <c1/>; RMC status <c2/> (and its mode indicator <c3/> and navigational status <c4/> where present); GNS mode <c5/> in every position; GLL status <c6/> and mode <c7/>; VTG mode <c8/> where it has one. The position fields are left as they were: the point is that the equipment sees the receiver itself declare the fix invalid and raises its own alarm. Every rewritten sentence has its checksum recomputed.

# text
# c1: [live] gate_release_s
# c2: --json <path>
seg-23ee46747297 = Once withheld, the fix is released only after the epochs have been out of the untrusted band for <c1/> (default { "30" } s), so a score at the band edge cannot make the fix flicker. The JSON lines go only where <c2/> says (by default nowhere, because the stream has stdout).

# text
seg-6b7b557a4909 = To let a chart plotter connect directly, serve the gated stream over TCP instead of stdout:

# text
# c1: --listen tcp:<port>
# c2: tcp:<addr>:<port>
# c3: --listen
# c4: --gate
# c5: --json -
seg-96ff51df9abe = <c1/> listens on the loopback address; <c2/> listens elsewhere (a warning is printed, because anyone who can reach the address can read the stream). Any number of clients may connect; each gets the same stream from the moment it connects, and clients only read. Every client has its own bounded queue and writer, so one that cannot keep up is dropped (its connection closed, a line on stderr) and never delays the others or the input. <c3/> needs <c4/>; stdout then carries nothing of the stream, so <c5/> is free.

# text
seg-960e0abb0466 = What the gate does not do, and what to know before using it:

# text
# c1: --gate
seg-ee3f18efa84c = It is <b>opt-in</b>. Nothing marks a fix invalid unless <c1/> is given.

# text
# c1: idle_flush_s
seg-9cd8243cb437 = It <b>adds latency</b>: a cycle is held until its epoch is scored, which is the time to the next timed sentence (about one cycle: a second for a { "1" } Hz receiver), or <c1/> after the last one when the stream stalls. Equipment that needs a sentence within a few tens of milliseconds of the receiver's output must not sit behind it.

# text
seg-ec63f8f3230b = It is <b>a single point in the chain</b>: if it stops, the stream stops. Run it supervised, and decide in advance what the downstream equipment does without a stream.

# text
# c1: note
seg-536e41a6a5e7 = It <b>fails closed on its own faults</b>: if the monitors cannot produce a score after calibration (a declared heading sensor that sends nothing, too few calibration epochs), the epoch is untrusted, carries a <c1/>, and is withheld; the release hold is timed on this computer's clock, not on the receiver's time, which is what a spoofer controls (for a replay there is only the receiver's time).

# text
seg-5dd62cd853c6 = It can <b>withhold a good fix</b> (a false alarm) as well as pass a spoofed one (a miss). The monitors check a fix against a ship's physics and its other sensors; a counterfeit that is consistent with all of them is not seen (see the limits below).

# text
# c1: $PKSHT
seg-a284a3ec9dfe = It expects a <b>pure NMEA { "0183" } text stream</b>. Binary frames mixed into the stream are forwarded unchanged, but a <c1/> can then land between the bytes of a frame.

# text
seg-0c919698fa92 = It is <b>not type-approved</b> and nothing in it has been assessed against IEC { "61108" } or IEC { "61162" }. The operator remains responsible for the navigation of the vessel.

